%% ============================================================================
%% Enterprise Customer Relationship Management Platform
%% EML — complete model: ERD · business rules · workflows
%%
%% Every rule, state and saga diagram below is stock Mermaid and renders as
%% drawn. The ERD carries EML's OPTIONAL modifier, which Mermaid's ER grammar
%% has no equivalent for, so it renders through toRenderableMermaid()
%% (packages/web/src/lib/mermaid-render.ts) — the normalization the application
%% applies to every ERD before handing it to Mermaid.
%%
%% Scope: the whole commercial lifecycle, front to back —
%%   marketing   Campaign, CampaignMember, Lead
%%   sales       Account, Contact, Opportunity, OpportunityLineItem, Product
%%   commerce    Quote, QuoteLineItem, Contract
%%   service     SupportCase, SlaPolicy
%%   operations  User, Team, Territory, Activity
%%
%% Generate:
%%   bun run generate:tanstack -- -i language/examples/crm.eml.mmd \
%%     -o generated-projects/crm -n crm
%%
%% Check:
%%   bun language/checker.ts language/examples/crm.eml.mmd
%%
%% ---- How the three layers fit together --------------------------------------
%% Structure is the ERD in Section 1. Everything after it is behaviour, and the
%% file is arranged so behaviour reads in the order it fires:
%%
%%   rules (2-9)      declarative decisions — scoring, routing, forecasting,
%%                    discount policy, triage, renewal risk. Compiled to GoRules
%%                    decision graphs and evaluated around the write.
%%   state (10-14)    the lifecycle each revenue object moves through, and who
%%                    is allowed to move it.
%%   hooks (15-21)    imperative side effects bound to CRUD lifecycle events.
%%   sagas (22-26)    multi-step, multi-entity processes: lead conversion,
%%                    discount approval, case escalation, closed-won handoff,
%%                    renewal playbook.
%%
%% Three of the sagas are `trigger: rule` — they run only when a rule's
%% %%action names them, so the rule's condition is what decides. The other two
%% run on the entity's own lifecycle. Both halves of that pairing are here on
%% purpose: it is the difference between "escalate when the discount is out of
%% policy" and "hand off every opportunity that closes".
%% ============================================================================

